{
  "description": "issue #954: each handler firing names **its own** block-head argument, not a constant and not a sibling's. Four handlers are registered -- two `on_key` for DIFFERENT keys (`space`, `enter`) and two `every` with DIFFERENT intervals (2, 3) -- and all four fire during one `wait 3`, with `space` delivered at tick 1 and `enter` at tick 2. That multiplicity is the discriminating power: a single-handler fixture passes even if the payload hardcodes its argument, reads the wrong handler's argument, or reports the first registration's argument for every firing, whereas here each of the four firings must report a value unique to itself. Tick 1 delivers the `space` press (`{kind: \"on_key\", key: \"space\"}`); tick 2 delivers `enter` and brings `every 2` due, in the normative same-tick order `on_key` before `every` (`spec/interaction-events.md`, ss.Time, ticks, and handlers) so `{key: \"enter\"}` precedes `{kind: \"every\", interval: 2}`; tick 3 brings `every 3` due (`{interval: 3}`). Each `every`'s first run is anchored to registration + interval, so interval 2 fires at tick 2 and interval 3 at tick 3, and neither is re-measured from when an invocation finished (`spec/interaction-events.md:244-248`). The printed values (1/2/3/4) pin the same ordering independently of the payloads, so a run that attributed a firing to the wrong handler fails on both assertions rather than on one. Registration `instruction` events for all four heads carry no `handler` key at all -- registrations are ordinary statements -- which is what makes the field the registration-versus-firing discriminator without any span comparison.",
